ion. The variable `same_file_reached_twice` and the MC/DC ignore note say the same; REVIEW-261003-GFB2 is the spec review. This matches the code (bin/omarchy-menu-file:55) and the bot and author comments on the PR. `observed_pass` (3 passed); error_handling `observed_pass`. Witnesses: link root plus target (line 105) and a root given twice each give one row; a missing path next to a real root is refused (line 141). The different-name hard-link case is witnessed only by the PR's own row 128. Status `review` / `in_review`.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":8},{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":21},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":105},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"severity":"medium","title":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y (added): one row per file reached twice under the same name, stated for cc710e11","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y is in review.changes (added, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"The script changed under an unchanged HR29; the trace re-review restamped it (bf56ddd8, our proof work; `review.traceReviewRestamped`). `observed_pass` (2 passed). Its error_handling cell (inherited from SYS-REQ-260922-6642) is `observed_pass` via the `refuse()` rows (menu-file-test.sh:132). Its own external_call_timeout_bounded cell is `not_linked`; review.changes lists the class under `acceptedKiDebt`, tracked by KI-MENU-SELECT-POLL-DEADLOCK (HR29 spec line 87), which pre-dates this PR.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":132},{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-260922-HR29.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":87}],"severity":"info","title":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 (code_changed, preserved): formats, dotfiles, mtime order, missing-path rejection","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 is in review.changes (code_changed, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$root\" \"jpg\" where $root/a/x.jpg and $root/b/x.jpg are hard links to one inode: base offers both paths, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Re-run locally with bfs and a stub selector: `r/a/x.jpg` and its hard link `r/b/x.jpg` under one root give two rows at the baseline, one row (`r/a/x.jpg`) at 03e6af6a and at cc710e11. The bot's comment on the PR names this case and leaves it as is (both paths hold the same content; the parent folder's identity is not available from one `find -printf`); the author agreed, and 9H5Y's rationale records it. So this is an accepted limitation, not a defect. A test that pins it would make a later change of the key visible. Wording note: 9H5Y says one find call 'cannot tell the parent directories apart'; find can print the parent path, but not whether two parent paths are the same folder, which is the point.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":21},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Known limitation: same-name hard links in different folders give one row; no test pins it","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Same-name hard links in two folders\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file","bin/omarchy-transcode"],"scenario":"omarchy-transcode with ~/Videos a symlink to a tree containing a mode-000 subdirectory: the user picks a file, and omarchy-menu-file exits 1 under pipefail so omarchy-transcode aborts"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Under `set -euo pipefail` the pipeline status is the rightmost non-zero one. `find -H ... 2>/dev/null` exits 1 on Permission denied but still prints the readable rows. The selector returns the pick, the script exits 1, and `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` (`input=$(omarchy-menu-file ...)` under `set -e`) aborts after the pick. This already happens for a real-directory root at the baseline. -H makes it reachable through link roots (e.g. `~/Videos -> /mnt/disk` with a root-owned `lost+found`), which listed nothing before. Re-run locally this round with bfs: a link root to a tree with a mode-000 directory lists nothing and exits 0 with the baseline script; at cc710e11 (as at 03e6af6a) the stub selector gets the pick and the script exits 1. FFTQ's hazard review names it as open and not covered. The PR's interior-loop row covers loops, not permissions.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: can a pick succeed and the script still exit 1 when a followed symlink root contains an unreadable directory?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"two files with identical mtime under one root, and aliased roots Pictures->Videos: compare row order and the surviving path spelling at base and head"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Baseline `sort -rn` reverses the whole-line tiebreak, so equal-mtime rows were ordered by path descending. `sort -t$'\\t' -k2,2rn` applies `r` to the key only, so ties fall back to an ascending whole-line comparison that starts with `D:i`. Re-run locally at cc710e11 with bfs: two equal-mtime files list b, a at the baseline and a, b at head; roots `V:P` and `P:V` (P a link to V) keep `P/c.jpg` in either order, where the baseline listed only `V/c.jpg`. 9H5Y's hazard review says the spelling depends on the sort order of equal rows; HR29 states mtime-descending only. No test pins the spelling the picker returns or the tie order.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: which alias spelling survives the dedup, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$d:$tmp/alias.jpg\" \"jpg\" where alias.jpg is a symlink to $d/p.jpg: baseline offers one row, cc710e11 offers two"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"`-H` dereferences every start point. A root that is a link to a matching regular file was not listed at the baseline; it is listed now. cc710e11 strips the first two tab fields before taking the name, so a bare-file root given next to its own folder dedups (re-run: `bf:bf/p.jpg` gives one row at head, two at the baseline). But a link `alias.jpg -> bf/p.jpg` given next to `bf` now gives two rows for one file (`alias.jpg`, `bf/p.jpg`): one row (`alias.jpg`) at 03e6af6a, one row (`bf/p.jpg` only) at the baseline. This follows 9H5Y's rule (different name, separate row), but neither the PR nor FFTQ speaks of file roots; FFTQ's hazard review notes the listing. No test covers it.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed, and as a second row when its name differs from the target's?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Symlink-to-file root next to its target's folder\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"a4f40d06 (our proof work) wrote the note (9H5Y line 96): awk keys on `%D:%i` plus the base name taken after the first two tab fields, so a tab in a file name stays in the name; a newline splits a row, as at the base. This matches `$1 \"/\" name` on line 55. Re-run locally: `a<TAB>b.jpg` is offered intact and stays a separate row from its hard link `c.jpg`; a newline name splits into fragments at the baseline and at head. One detail the note leaves out: at head the fragments also pass through the dedup, so two different files `d1/a<NL>x.jpg` and `d2/a<NL>x.jpg` give one `x.jpg` fragment where the baseline gave two. Both outputs are unusable at the baseline too, so this is not a defect. The trace and lifecycle records that name head 03e6af6a are history; the cc710e11 re-review follows them.","refs":[{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":96},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"9H5Y's structural hazard note states the cc710e11 key","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"review.compat is `compatible`: test_diff, mcdc_diff (87 -> 89 decisions), removed_behavior and differential were all checked, with 0 findings and 213 inputs over 5 partitions. The configured harness `test/bdiff/harness.mjs` covers only `shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js` and `Menu.qml`, and the package's dependencies record lists only menu corpus files under the harness role, so none of those inputs ran this script; `review.behaviorDiff.inventory` is empty. The removed expressions (`find` default -P, `-printf '%T@\\t%p\\n'`, `sort -rn`, `cut -f2-`) were never run base-vs-head by the harness; the questions above are what such a run would settle. test_diff reports no existing test file modified or deleted, although our proof work edits `menu-file-test.sh` (rows and comments added only). Context, not an approval.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":47},{"id":"test/bdiff/harness.mjs","kind":"file"}],"severity":"info","title":"Compatibility 'compatible' carries no differential evidence for bin/omarchy-menu-file","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`coverage_unmeasured`: `review.diffCoverage.unmeasuredFiles` = [bin/omarchy-menu-file]. The 0 uncovered lines/outcomes says nothing about lines 47-56. The bash coverage target for this script, `menu-bash-file` (proof.yaml:531), is `enabled: false`. `effects_unavailable`: `review.effects` is absent (call-level effects are not analyzed for bash), so no new-call, gained-effect or caller facts exist, and no uncodified connection could be detected (review.scope approximations). Callers found by reading: `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` and `bin/omarchy-games-retro-install:22` (`|| exit 0`); both use the picked name, which is why cc710e11 keeps hard-linked names apart.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":531},{"id":"bin/omarchy-games-retro-install","kind":"file","line":22}],"severity":"info","title":"Changed-code coverage of the script was not measured; effects were not analyzed","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The script's two self-skips (`no GNU find`, `find lacks -printf`) call base-test.sh `pass` (not `skip`) and exit 0, so the JUnit testcase would record a pass with nothing run. On this run the evidence points to a real run: `menu-file-test.sh` sits in the same suite behind a GNU find -printf gate that records a JUnit skip (proof.yaml:215), and it ran and passed; testResults show 59/59 passed, 0 skipped. Upstream could use `skip` (`# SKIP`), which test/junit/live-session.sh already treats as a skip.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":11},{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":215},{"id":"test/junit/live-session.sh","kind":"file","line":129}],"severity":"info","title":"The PR's test passes on a self-skip too (opinion)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The body still says 'Dedup rows after `cut -f2-` with `awk '!seen[$0]++'`'. Head cc710e11 keys on device:inode plus the file name before `cut -f3-`, and the same-name hard-link limitation lives only in a PR comment. Worth updating so the merged history describes the shipped mechanism and its limitation.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":52}],"severity":"info","title":"PR description does not match the head (opinion)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`-printf` was already GNU-only, and `%D`/`%i` are also GNU (both test scripts gate on GNU find). The `checks_skipped` disclosure (13 gates, e.g. documentation_coverage, security_surface_covered) is configuration, not caused by this PR. Any external auditor assessment recorded on this package is advisory: a note, not an approval.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":10},{"id":"documentation_coverage","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"Pre-existing: GNU find dependency; 13 checks skipped by configuration","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"Our proof work added `refuse()` to `menu-file-test.sh` and traced it with `# Verifies: SW-REQ-260922-HR29, SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ, SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y` on line 120. review.scope lists it as an added unit with a function-level mapping and no flags. It runs the real script, asserts exit 1, the `Path not found` message, and that the selector is never reached. It is called for a missing path (HR29, line 132), a link root whose target is gone (FFTQ, line 137) and a missing path next to a real root (9H5Y, line 141); these rows are the error_handling witnesses behind the three `observed_pass` cells.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":121},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"The refuse() test helper is traced at function level to all three requirements","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The upstream change and our proof commits were replayed onto 311d29c7 without edits (only the Proof-Baseline trailer and the record commit differ). `bin/omarchy-menu-file`, its callers (`bin/omarchy-transcode`, `bin/omarchy-games-retro-install`) and both tests are byte-identical to the previous evaluation; review.changes, diff, diffCoverage, tests and risk are unchanged. The baseline merged upstream quattro 393a43d4 (#6951, `bin/omarchy-upgrade-to-quattro`), and added proof work outside this script: the theme remover, `shell/services/AppSearch.js` and `shell/plugins/emojis/EmojiSearch.js` in the menu verification scope (SW-REQ-261003-C9GM, SW-REQ-261004-JATY), a known issue for Remove > Theme, the `plugins-shell` test command and three tests (59 test cases, was 56), and a menu variable budget of 140 (was 130). The dependencies record counts 131 of 140 menu variables in use: 127 at the baseline plus this change's 4 (`symlink_root_given`, `symlink_root_listed`, `same_file_reached_twice`, `file_listed_once`), so the raise is what keeps proof_complexity_clean passing for this mirror. The gate passed at the baseline and at head (`delta` unchanged).","refs":[{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":328},{"id":"specs/software/variables/menu.vars.yaml","kind":"file","line":399},{"id":"proof_complexity_clean","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"Baseline move 87c3ca68 -> 311d29c7 leaves this change's code and evidence unchanged","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}}],"packageDigest":"sha256:da45cb5dd648b94e7c110af64f8485c87ebc1c8e01983ad80d42b987c99982a0","packageIdentity":"epkg-0b39e5a924fca56f1ad6709e9dab7e400829dc696651f1a6dd721fa2df74d94e","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"A small, well-motivated pipeline change, evaluated at upstream head cc710e11 on baseline 311d29c7. Its purpose is codified (FFTQ and 9H5Y, which state the cc710e11 head, key and the same-name hard-link limitation) and observed passing, with witnesses that fail on the baseline; the PR's new hard-link row fails on the previous head. Coverage of the script was not measured, effects were not analyzed, and the differential harness does not run the script. Untested edges remain: pipefail exit 1 via an unreadable directory under a followed root (named open in FFTQ), tie order and alias spelling, symlink-to-file roots, the pinned same-name limitation."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":4,"rationale":"Pin the accepted limitation (one row) so a later change of the dedup key is visible; 9H5Y's rationale is the intent.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Same-name hard links in two folders"},{"forClaim":5,"rationale":"mkdir a mode-000 dir under the link target (run as non-root), pick a file with a stub selector, and assert omarchy-menu-file's exit status (and omarchy-transcode's).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169}],"title":"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root"},{"forClaim":6,"rationale":"touch -d the same time on two files and on an aliased pair, then assert row order and which path the stub selector receives, at base and head.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling"},{"forClaim":7,"rationale":"Pass a folder and a differently named symlink to a file in it; assert whether the file is offered once or twice.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54}],"title":"Symlink-to-file root next to its target's folder"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #13197 (Bartok9, part of #12665) makes `bin/omarchy-menu-file` list files under symlinked roots, so `omarchy transcode` gets a picker when `~/Videos` is a link. It runs `find -H` (start points followed, links inside a root not descended) and drops repeated rows after the mtime sort, keyed on device:inode plus the file name, so hard links under different names stay separate rows (head cc710e11). The PR test has 9 rows. The evaluated tree af9b69ff is baseline quattro-proof 311d29c7, plus the upstream change (f4a772df), plus our proof work (bf56ddd8..a4f40d06): SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ and -9H5Y, witnesses in `menu-file-test.sh`, spec reviews. Upstream change and proof work were replayed unchanged from 87c3ca68.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** The package says readiness `ready`; next decision `human_judgement` (acceptance) by a maintainer. Disclosures: 3, all inform (coverage_unmeasured, checks_skipped, effects_unavailable). FFTQ, 9H5Y and HR29 are `observed_pass`; testResults: 59 passed, 0 failed, current. Coverage of the script was not measured and effects were not analyzed. Compatibility is `compatible` (213 inputs, 0 findings), but the differential harness never runs this script. Re-run locally with bfs (GNU-compatible, not GNU find): the PR test passes 9/9 on cc710e11, its hard-link row fails on 03e6af6a, its first row fails on the baseline script.\n\n**Baseline move 87c3ca68 -> 311d29c7.** `bin/omarchy-menu-file`, its callers and tests are unchanged. The move adds tests (56 -> 59), new menu scope, and raises the menu variable budget to 140; this mirror uses 131.\n\n**Questions for a human.** Can a pick under a followed root holding an unreadable directory still exit 1 under pipefail? Which alias spelling survives, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted? Is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed, and as a second row when its name differs? No test in the package covers these.\n\n**Traceability.** The script and both tests trace to FFTQ, 9H5Y and HR29. No spec-gap candidates; scope coherence `single`.\n\n**Not caused by this PR.** 13 checks skipped by configuration; the GNU find dependency; newline names split rows; the HR29 timeout debt. This advisory review is not an approval."}
